{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And thirty days and three days.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the days of purity were thirty and three, so that together with the seven days of impurity they would be forty days, corresponding to the number of days in which a male is completed in his mother's womb. And by way of the Kabbalah: thirty-three days of purity were given to the woman, corresponding to the Bride in the Song of Songs, who is comprised of the thirty-two paths of wisdom; and with them there will be union, and therefore they were thirty and three days and not thirty and two, alluding to the Bride who joins with wisdom — she bearing those thirty and two and being reckoned together with them." "<b>She shall dwell.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, explained that it is not an expression of sitting but an expression of waiting, as in (Deuteronomy 1:46) ״ותשבו בקדש ימים רבים״. And the measure of the verse is: thirty days and three days she shall wait, before she touches any consecrated thing and before she comes to the sanctuary, even though they are days of purity with respect to her husband. And this is the meaning of 'in the blood of her purity' — to say that even though she sees and is pure. And the Ramban, of blessed memory, explained 'she shall dwell' as an expression of conjugal relations, as in (Hosea 3:3) ״ימים רבים תשבי לי לא תזני ולא תהיי לאיש״, for a woman who lies with her husband is called one who 'dwells' with him. And since with regard to the seven days it says ״וטמאה שבעת ימים כימי נדת דותה״ — she shall be impure to her husband and to consecrated things for all seven days, it says that after the seven she may 'dwell' with her husband for thirty and three days in the blood of purity; but she shall not touch consecrated things and shall not enter the sanctuary even if she does not see blood, yet she may 'dwell' with her husband even if she sees. For our Rabbis, of blessed memory, have a tradition that a woman who gives birth is pure after the seven days for ordinary matters and for her husband, when she counts seven clean days — and this is what is meant by 'in the seven, as in the days of the impurity of her menstruation, she shall be impure'; and regarding these days it says she shall be impure to consecrated things and to the sanctuary, but not for ordinary matters and not for her husband." "<b>And thus</b> wrote the Rambam, of blessed memory, in the Book of Holiness, chapter 11, and this is his language: 'That which you find in some places, and which you find in the responsa of some of the Geonim, of blessed memory, that a woman who gives birth to a male may not consort with her husband until the end of forty days, and one who gives birth to a female until the end of eighty — even though she did not see blood except during the seven days — this is not a custom but an error in those responsa, and a way of sectarianism in those places; and they learned this matter from the Sadducees. And it is a commandment to protest against them, in order to remove from their hearts the practice of the Sadducees and to uphold the words of the Sages, that she counts only seven clean days, as we have explained.'" "<b>In the blood of her purity.</b> Blood that is pure, and it is the counterpart of the blood of menstruation, and it does not cause impurity." "<b>And the sage R. Abraham, of blessed memory, wrote:</b> HaShem decreed upon the male according to the number of days in which his form is completed in the womb, and upon the female double, for she is completed at eighty; therefore the impurity of the female lasts twice as long, and the purity likewise lasts twice as long." "<b>But</b> the view of our Rabbis of blessed memory is that the form is completed at forty days, whether for a male or for a female. And the reason for the doubling, according to their opinion, is because the nature of the female is cold and moist, and she requires extensive purification because of the abundance of moistness implanted in her; therefore she needs to wait all this time so that she may be cleansed from the blood of birth, and from the womb, and from pregnancy — for thus people whose nature is cold and moist require a longer time for their purification than those whose nature is hot."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:13.194362103Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}